WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.280
Light an oat seedling from one side, and it bends toward the lamp.

2
00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:11.720
The tip senses the light, yet the bend forms lower down.

3
00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:14.000
What carries the message?

4
00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:19.110
Picture the young shoot as two columns of cells, one on each side.

5
00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:21.590
Each side is called a flank.

6
00:00:22.683 --> 00:00:26.563
If both flanks lengthen by the same amount, the shoot grows taller.

7
00:00:27.763 --> 00:00:30.583
But it stays perfectly straight, however much it grows.

8
00:00:31.733 --> 00:00:35.353
Now let the shaded flank lengthen a little more than the lit flank.

9
00:00:36.653 --> 00:00:38.113
The shoot curves toward the light.

10
00:00:39.353 --> 00:00:41.293
A bend is simply a difference in growth.

11
00:00:42.383 --> 00:00:44.223
So what speeds the shaded flank?

12
00:00:45.643 --> 00:00:50.143
Charles Darwin and his son Francis lit canary-grass seedlings from one side.

13
00:00:51.363 --> 00:00:54.123
An opaque cap over the tip greatly reduced the bending.

14
00:00:55.267 --> 00:00:56.967
The lower part was still lit.

15
00:00:58.147 --> 00:01:02.647
So light is sensed mainly near the tip, and something passes down to where bending

16
00:01:02.647 --> 00:01:03.647
happens.

17
00:01:04.217 --> 00:01:09.557
Peter Boysen-Jensen used oat coleoptiles, the sheath over a grass seedling's first

18
00:01:09.557 --> 00:01:16.077
leaf. He slipped thin mica, which dissolved chemicals cannot cross, into a cut below

19
00:01:16.077 --> 00:01:17.077
the tip.

20
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:21.160
With mica on the shaded side, the coleoptile stayed straight.

21
00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:24.740
With mica on the lit side, it still bent.

22
00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:30.000
Boysen-Jensen concluded that the signal runs down the shaded side.

23
00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:34.890
Frits Went then stood cut tips on small blocks of agar, a gel.

24
00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:41.430
In darkness, he set one block on one side of a coleoptile whose own tip was cut off.

25
00:01:42.483 --> 00:01:44.863
The coleoptile curved away from the block.

26
00:01:46.103 --> 00:01:48.783
Something from the tips had sped growth beneath it.

27
00:01:49.723 --> 00:01:51.603
We now call that activity auxin.

28
00:01:52.567 --> 00:01:55.527
Why does the shaded flank end up with more auxin?

29
00:01:56.947 --> 00:01:58.827
Light could destroy auxin on the lit side.

30
00:01:59.967 --> 00:02:02.747
Or light could move auxin across to the shaded side.

31
00:02:03.767 --> 00:02:10.107
Here is the test. Collect all the auxin from tips lit on one side, and from tips

32
00:02:10.107 --> 00:02:15.127
kept dark. If light only moves auxin, how should the totals compare?

33
00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:26.370
Briggs and colleagues split tips with a thin barrier and caught each half in its own

34
00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:30.330
block. Lit tips gave the same total as dark tips.

35
00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:33.070
But more came from the shaded half.

36
00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:39.220
So in these grass tips, the unchanged total supports auxin moving to the shaded

37
00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:41.360
side, rather than being destroyed.

38
00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:46.760
The auxin-rich shaded flank grew faster, and the shoot bent toward the light.

39
00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.400
Growth follows the auxin level, but not in strict proportion.

40
00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:53.940
It rises to a peak.

41
00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:57.500
Past that peak, more auxin slows growth.

42
00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:00.730
So a bend is a difference in growth.

43
00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:05.390
Light makes that difference by moving auxin to the shaded flank.
